The AGPL is a strong copyleft licence with one property that matters here: it
covers use *over a network*. If you run docier as part of a service that users
interact with remotely, the AGPL requires you to offer those users the
Corresponding Source of the covered work. A plain GPL would not, which is why
the AGPL is the licence used for this half of the dual licence.

================================================================================
2. COMMERCIAL — a commercial licence
================================================================================

If you want to use docier in a proprietary product, or in a network service
whose source you do not wish to publish, the AGPL does not fit. A commercial
licence is available that permits exactly that, without the copyleft
obligations. See COMMERCIAL.md.

You do not need a commercial licence to:
  - use docier in an open-source project under the AGPL;
  - evaluate docier, or use it in development and testing;
  - read, study and modify the source for your own understanding.

You do need one to ship docier inside a closed-source product or a closed
network service.

